<mujoco model="scene_desktop">
    <!-- The desktop pet's world: a strip of invisible floor pinned to the top
         of the Dock, walls at the mapped screen edges, and nothing else the
         camera is allowed to see but the duck.

         NOT DIRECTLY LOADABLE — this is a template. `@ROBOT_XML@` and
         `@MESHDIR@` are substituted with absolute paths under --rl-repo at
         load time (sim_server.local_scene_xml). MuJoCo resolves `<include>`
         relative to the INCLUDING file but `meshdir` relative to the MAIN
         file, so a scene living outside microduck_rl has to say both, and
         only the daemon knows where the clone is.

         WHAT THE PET RENDERER SEES (sim_server._handle_pet_frame):
           group 0/1/2  drawn  -> the duck (visual geoms are group 2) and the
                                  chroma backdrop
           group 4      hidden -> floor, walls, depth rails, platform boxes.
                                  Hidden, not deleted: they are fully
                                  collidable, they just never occlude the duck
                                  in either the RGB or the segmentation pass.
                                  (Group >= 3 is off in MuJoCo's default
                                  MjvOption too, so the AX debug page on a pet
                                  daemon shows a floating duck. That is the
                                  scene being honest, not a bug.)

         GEOMETRY IS SET AT RUNTIME, NOT HERE. Everything positional below is
         a placeholder: `pet_config` moves the walls to the screen edges and
         `pet_world` moves and resizes the platform boxes. No recompile, no
         restart, so one daemon survives a Dock resize and the window-ledge
         lane repositioning ledges every frame.

         WHY THEY ARE MOCAP BODIES AND NOT PLAIN WORLD GEOMS. Writing
         model.geom_pos on a geom that hangs directly off the worldbody moves
         it in the renderer and in mjData.geom_xpos, and then it QUIETLY STOPS
         COLLIDING: the world body's bounding-volume hierarchy is built at
         compile time and a geom parked out at y=+10 keeps a leaf out at y=+10
         no matter where its pos says it is. Measured on this scene — a duck
         dropped from 0.6 m onto a 0.35 x 0.30 x 0.15 m "ledge" moved directly
         beneath it fell straight through, with not one contact in 14 seconds.
         A mocap body is the classic fix and the only one that is honest here:
         it is its own body, posed from mjData.mocap_pos, and its bounds are
         recomputed every step. Same experiment, mocap body, contact on the
         first bounce.

         So: POSITION is mjData.mocap_pos[body_mocapid]; SIZE is
         model.geom_size, plus geom_rbound and geom_aabb, which MuJoCo derives
         from the size at compile time and would otherwise go stale (a box you
         grew stops generating contacts outside its old bounding sphere).
         Neither is touched by `reset`, which only rewinds qpos/qvel — the
         screen the duck lives on survives a new episode, as it should.

         WHY ORTHOGRAPHIC, AND WHY IT IS NOT SET HERE. An orthographic free
         camera makes the pet frame a pixel-exact map of the world:
         metres-per-pixel is fovy/height everywhere in frame, at every depth
         (measured: exact to the pixel, independent of the camera distance).
         That is what lets the sim floor BE the Dock's top edge and a platform
         box BE a window's title bar, rather than approximately be them near
         the middle of the screen. But `orthographic` and `fovy` live on
         mjModel.vis.global_, which every renderer on this model shares — and
         under orthographic, fovy stops meaning degrees and starts meaning
         metres, which would leave the AX debug page's `distance` knob doing
         nothing. So the pet renderer sets both from px_per_meter around its
         own two render calls and puts them back (all renders run on the sim
         thread, so nothing can observe the model mid-swap), and this scene
         stays a perspective scene for `duck cam` and the debug page.

         `<statistic>` is pinned so parked platform boxes (out at y = +10)
         cannot inflate the model extent — near/far clipping is derived from
         it, and an inflated extent silently clips the duck out of frame. -->

    <include file="@ROBOT_XML@"/>
    <compiler angle="radian" meshdir="@MESHDIR@"/>

    <statistic center="0 0 0.15" extent="0.6"/>

    <visual>
        <!-- Shadows cost ~6 ms of the 20 ms control period and land on a floor
             nobody can see. MSAA is off for a subtler reason: the pet frame is
             supersampled 2x and box-filtered, which is already antialiasing,
             and multisampled RGB edges are blended with the backdrop while the
             segmentation mask that keys the alpha is perfectly binary — so
             every silhouette subpixel comes back part magenta with alpha 255,
             and premultiplication cannot undo what the GPU already mixed.
             Hard edges at 2x, averaged down, are both cheaper and cleaner. -->
        <headlight diffuse="0.62 0.62 0.63" ambient="0.42 0.43 0.45" specular="0.10 0.10 0.10"/>
        <quality shadowsize="0" offsamples="0"/>
        <!-- Fog is pushed past the far clip. MuJoCo's default fogstart/fogend
             are 3 and 10 model extents (1.8 m and 6 m here), which is close
             enough to haze the backdrop into a grey gradient — and a hazed
             backdrop is no longer a chroma key. haze is magenta too, so even
             a residual blend lands on the key colour rather than off it.
             `fovy` is left at MuJoCo's 45 degrees: the pet renderer overwrites
             it per frame with a view height in metres, and a placeholder left
             here would be read as 0.46 DEGREES by every perspective render on
             this model (measured: the AX debug page becomes a telephoto
             sliver of backdrop). -->
        <map znear="0.005" fogstart="30" fogend="60"/>
        <rgba haze="1 0 1 1"/>
        <global azimuth="90" elevation="0" offwidth="1024" offheight="1024"/>
    </visual>

    <asset>
        <!-- Flat, unlit, and a colour no part of the duck contains: the
             chroma-key fallback for when segmentation rendering is
             unavailable. In the normal path the segmentation mask keys the
             alpha and this is just the background the RGB pass happens to
             have. emission="1" keeps it exactly (255, 0, 255) under any
             lighting, so a fallback key can be a hard equality test. -->
        <material name="pet_chroma" rgba="1 0 1 1" emission="1"
            specular="0" shininess="0" reflectance="0"/>
    </asset>

    <default>
        <!-- The invisible world: collides with everything, drawn by nothing.
             Same friction as the stock ground plane. -->
        <default class="pet_solid">
            <geom type="box" group="4" rgba="0.4 0.4 0.45 1" condim="3"/>
        </default>
    </default>

    <worldbody>
        <!-- One soft key light from the viewer's side so the duck reads as a
             sticker on the Dock rather than a flat cutout. No shadow caster. -->
        <light name="pet_key" directional="true" castshadow="false"
            pos="-0.6 -1.4 1.8" dir="0.25 0.62 -1"
            diffuse="0.52 0.52 0.50" specular="0.10 0.10 0.10"/>
        <light name="pet_fill" directional="true" castshadow="false"
            pos="0.8 0.9 1.2" dir="-0.3 -0.5 -1"
            diffuse="0.16 0.17 0.20" specular="0 0 0"/>

        <!-- The Dock's top edge. An ordinary infinite ground plane; the app
             pins world z=0 to it. Invisible (group 4), fully collidable. -->
        <geom name="floor" size="0 0 0.05" pos="0 0 0" type="plane" group="4"
            rgba="0.3 0.3 0.35 1" condim="3"/>

        <!-- The chroma backdrop, behind everything the camera can reach. -->
        <geom name="pet_backdrop" type="box" pos="0 1.60 0.60" size="8 0.01 3"
            material="pet_chroma" contype="0" conaffinity="0" group="1"/>

        <!-- SCREEN EDGES. x is moved to +/- (screen_width_m/2 - margin) by
             pet_config; these placeholders are one default 1728 pt screen at
             656 px/m with a 300 pt window. 0.6 m tall so a stagger cannot top
             them, 1.2 m deep in y so a diagonal cannot round them, and sunk
             0.02 m below the floor so no toe can catch the seam. (A mocap
             body overlapping the ground plane costs nothing: MuJoCo never
             generates contacts between two bodies that both have no dofs.)
             Body and geom share a name — different namespaces, one lookup. -->
        <body name="pet_wall_left" mocap="true" pos="-1.089 0 0.28">
            <geom name="pet_wall_left" class="pet_solid" size="0.02 0.60 0.30"/>
        </body>
        <body name="pet_wall_right" mocap="true" pos="1.089 0 0.28">
            <geom name="pet_wall_right" class="pet_solid" size="0.02 0.60 0.30"/>
        </body>

        <!-- DEPTH RAILS. The pet frame is a side view: y is the camera axis,
             so drift in y is invisible on screen right up until the duck
             walks behind the backdrop and vanishes. These keep it in a
             corridor. Moved by pet_config's `corridor_m`. -->
        <body name="pet_rail_near" mocap="true" pos="0 -0.35 0.28">
            <geom name="pet_rail_near" class="pet_solid" size="4 0.02 0.30"/>
        </body>
        <body name="pet_rail_far" mocap="true" pos="0 0.35 0.28">
            <geom name="pet_rail_far" class="pet_solid" size="4 0.02 0.30"/>
        </body>

        <!-- THE PLATFORMING LANE (blueprint v2: real app windows as ledges).
             Twelve boxes pre-allocated because a rectangle that has to exist
             before it can be positioned is cheaper than a model recompile
             mid-session — and a recompile would drop the duck's state on the
             floor. Parked at y=+10: past the rails, out of every contact pair,
             and (thanks to the pinned <statistic>) unable to move the
             clipping planes. pet_world moves and resizes them; anything it is
             not given goes back to the parking lot. -->
        <body name="pet_platform_00" mocap="true" pos="0 10 0.5">
            <geom name="pet_platform_00" class="pet_solid" size="0.05 0.15 0.02"/>
        </body>
        <body name="pet_platform_01" mocap="true" pos="0 10 0.5">
            <geom name="pet_platform_01" class="pet_solid" size="0.05 0.15 0.02"/>
        </body>
        <body name="pet_platform_02" mocap="true" pos="0 10 0.5">
            <geom name="pet_platform_02" class="pet_solid" size="0.05 0.15 0.02"/>
        </body>
        <body name="pet_platform_03" mocap="true" pos="0 10 0.5">
            <geom name="pet_platform_03" class="pet_solid" size="0.05 0.15 0.02"/>
        </body>
        <body name="pet_platform_04" mocap="true" pos="0 10 0.5">
            <geom name="pet_platform_04" class="pet_solid" size="0.05 0.15 0.02"/>
        </body>
        <body name="pet_platform_05" mocap="true" pos="0 10 0.5">
            <geom name="pet_platform_05" class="pet_solid" size="0.05 0.15 0.02"/>
        </body>
        <body name="pet_platform_06" mocap="true" pos="0 10 0.5">
            <geom name="pet_platform_06" class="pet_solid" size="0.05 0.15 0.02"/>
        </body>
        <body name="pet_platform_07" mocap="true" pos="0 10 0.5">
            <geom name="pet_platform_07" class="pet_solid" size="0.05 0.15 0.02"/>
        </body>
        <body name="pet_platform_08" mocap="true" pos="0 10 0.5">
            <geom name="pet_platform_08" class="pet_solid" size="0.05 0.15 0.02"/>
        </body>
        <body name="pet_platform_09" mocap="true" pos="0 10 0.5">
            <geom name="pet_platform_09" class="pet_solid" size="0.05 0.15 0.02"/>
        </body>
        <body name="pet_platform_10" mocap="true" pos="0 10 0.5">
            <geom name="pet_platform_10" class="pet_solid" size="0.05 0.15 0.02"/>
        </body>
        <body name="pet_platform_11" mocap="true" pos="0 10 0.5">
            <geom name="pet_platform_11" class="pet_solid" size="0.05 0.15 0.02"/>
        </body>

        <!-- THE TOY. A real free body, not a prop: the same 70 mm, 15 g
             floorball that microduck_rl's ball.xml ships, down to the inertia,
             because sim_server.BALL_RADIUS_M (0.035) is baked into the head
             camera's range solve — the detector reads distance out of the
             SOLID ANGLE the blob covers, so a ball of any other size would
             report the wrong `ball_seen.distance_m` and the approach would
             stop short of a pocket it thought it was in.

             Group 0, unlike every other thing this file adds: the pet
             renderer draws groups 0/1/2, so the toy is on screen, and the
             head camera can find it. It is the first object in the pet's
             world that is neither duck nor invisible furniture, which is why
             sim_server splits its segmentation masks (`_pet_duck_geom` vs
             `_pet_ball_geom`) instead of asking "is it on the worldbody" —
             the frame's `bbox` is what the overlay hit-tests against, and a
             ball folded into it would be a duck you could grab from a metre
             away.

             `ball_geom` / `ball_free` are the names the rest of the daemon
             already looks up: `_fix_ball_contact` re-applies condim 6 and the
             rolling friction at load (they are written here so the file reads
             true), and infer_policy.PolicyInference finds `ball_free` to fill
             ball_qpos_adr / ball_qvel_adr — which is also what turns the head
             camera's detector on and what `push {target: "ball"}` writes to.

             It spawns at x=0.75, off to the right of a duck that starts at
             the origin and OUTSIDE the duck's own 0.39 m window: the toy is
             found by walking to it, not by being handed one. -->
        <body name="pet_ball" pos="0.75 0 0.035">
            <freejoint name="ball_free"/>
            <inertial pos="0 0 0" mass="0.015"
                diaginertia="1.225e-5 1.225e-5 1.225e-5"/>
            <geom type="sphere" name="ball_geom" size="0.035" group="0"
                rgba="1 0.55 0 1" condim="6" friction="0.5 0.005 0.002"/>
        </body>

        <!-- THE HAND. Invisible (group 4, culled by the pet renderer's option
             the same way the floor and the walls are) and non-colliding
             (contype/conaffinity 0): it is not a thing in the world, it is
             where the human's fingers are. Nothing may bump into it, nothing
             may stand on it, and the duck must never be able to see it.

             A mocap body for both of this file's reasons at once. The first
             is the one the walls are written against — mjData.mocap_pos is
             the only pose channel whose bounds stay honest, and the hand is
             moved every single tick while somebody is holding the duck. The
             second is new: a weld needs a body to weld TO, and a bare geom on
             the worldbody is not one.

             Parked at z=3, well above anything the pet camera can frame, so
             an idle hand is nowhere near the duck. That parking spot is also
             what makes the weld below wrong at compile time — see there. -->
        <body name="pet_hand" mocap="true" pos="0 0 3.0">
            <geom name="pet_hand" type="sphere" size="0.01" group="4"
                contype="0" conaffinity="0" rgba="0 0 0 0"/>
        </body>
    </worldbody>

    <!-- PICK-UP. Inactive at load and toggled from mjData.eq_active at
         runtime, so the duck is welded to the hand only for as long as
         somebody is actually holding the mouse button down on it.

         `relpose` is left at the default DELIBERATELY, and it is a trap worth
         spelling out. MuJoCo bakes a weld's relative pose from qpos0 at
         compile time, and qpos0 here is the hand parked at z=3 against a duck
         standing at z=0.12 — measured on this compiled scene, eq_data comes
         back [0,0,0, 0,0,-2.88, 1,0,0,0, 1]. Activating that unedited would
         snap the duck 2.88 m straight down, through the Dock, on the first
         step of every pick-up. So sim_server._pet_carry_grab rewrites
         eq_data[3:10] from the LIVE relative pose every time a hand closes,
         and what is written here is a placeholder exactly the way every
         position in this file is a placeholder.

         solref="0.02 1" is a measured choice, not a default: at that
         stiffness the trunk tracks the hand about 4 cm behind, which reads as
         a duck with weight in it rather than a duck on the end of a stick.
         Stiffen towards "0.005 1" if it ever looks glued. -->
    <equality>
        <weld name="pet_carry" body1="pet_hand" body2="trunk_base"
            active="false" solref="0.02 1" solimp="0.9 0.95 0.001"/>
    </equality>

    <!-- The stock STAND pose, copied from microduck_rl's scene.xml. The
         daemon never reads it (DuckSim sets qpos and ctrl programmatically at
         init, the same way infer_policy does), but a scene you can drop into
         mj_resetDataKeyframe and get a standing duck out of is worth the
         twenty lines — it is how tests/test_pet_stream.py checks that the sim
         floor lands on the Dock's edge without booting a policy.

         THE LAST SEVEN NUMBERS ARE THE BALL, and they are not optional.
         `ball_free` is a freejoint, so it owns qpos[21:28] — and a keyframe
         shorter than nq is ZERO-PADDED by the compiler, which would hand the
         ball the quaternion (0,0,0,0). A zero quat is not "no rotation", it
         is not a rotation at all: mj_forward normalises it to garbage and
         anything that copies this keyframe wholesale (tests/test_pet_stream's
         `_reset_pose` does exactly that) gets a ball that is nowhere, in an
         orientation that is nothing. Grow this string with the scene. -->
    <keyframe>
        <key name="STAND" qpos="
        0 0 0.12 1 0 0 0

        0 -0.08726646259971647 -0.457924 -0.004940 0.452984
        0.3490658503988659 0.3490658503988659 0 0
        0 0.08726646259971647 0.457924 0.004940 -0.452984

        0.75 0 0.035 1 0 0 0"
         ctrl="
        0 -0.08726646259971647 -0.457924 -0.004940 0.452984
        0.3490658503988659 0.3490658503988659 0 0
        0 0.08726646259971647 0.457924 0.004940 -0.452984"
        />
    </keyframe>
</mujoco>
